{
  "id": "8d975b9f-7f46-42f5-8366-093a1894bbce",
  "title": "Boundary Resolution — Choosing the Next Composition Step",
  "templateRef": {
    "id": "technical-design",
    "version": 2
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "id": "f2b3ea05-a13e-4875-a6dc-4871aea7ea9c",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Abstract",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "feb86104-63aa-411e-af1c-b22a32d59dba",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Working draft — Paper 2. We investigate how a generator chooses the next description-changing action when an intent is not yet supported by the available library and instance specification. The proposal is a boundary-resolution policy over explicit requirements, versioned descriptions, graph state, judgments, and gaps. This manuscript remains theoretical: its output is an updated instance specification and decision trace, not runtime execution. No new experiments, rendered figures, or claims of optimality are included.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "b3a80b2e-6d64-45bf-ab72-916936e79d94",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Research Question and Scope",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "8f130c58-bc71-4571-9a72-345c63e69581",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Given an intent, component descriptions, applicable rule descriptions, and unresolved gaps, which action should the generator propose next? The action may clarify intent, resolve a reference, decompose a requirement, reuse an existing item, connect items, package a higher-layer representation, revise a candidate, defer an obligation, or stop. Moving upward is one possible action, not a mandatory measure of progress.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "4b8c8b29-7afd-4c78-a69f-609d3cf9cf54",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Relationship to Paper 1",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ec9caf30-c6f9-4061-b12c-35f887789c8a",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Paper 1 defines descriptions, occurrences, assembly boundaries, representational packaging, and met/violated/unknown judgments. Paper 2 must define how those records guide a decision. It inherits the distinction between a structurally complete specification and proven behavior. A library item is not selected merely because its label sounds relevant, and a generated higher-layer description is not evidence that its claimed behavior works.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1c4f79e6-6aa7-4b72-9e62-78645be306ca",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Proposed Decision State",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "7171be83-0799-4f5c-bbf1-4ff9e3601e88",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Let S = (Rᵦ, Lᵥ, G, U, J, T, b): a versioned requirement baseline, a pinned library snapshot, the current multilayer graphs, active unresolved obligations, versioned judgments, decision history, and remaining budget. A boundary is an identified relation between a requirement and a missing or insufficiently justified realization. It records what is required, what is available, where they fail to meet, and which facts are unknown or contradicted. The boundary is not a vague global entropy score.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ab2fe7ec-5f1f-4f31-9d58-368ff164087d",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. Candidate Decision Procedure — Provisional",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "c9697b3c-a794-4aa2-9c7a-4c17207e31ed",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "First, select a requirement-scoped unresolved boundary. Second, identify applicable rule families using the descriptions and known gaps. Third, generate candidate actions with declared operands and expected specification changes. Fourth, reject candidates that violate mandatory conditions and preserve unknown conditions as obligations. Fifth, compare the remaining candidates under an explicit policy. Sixth, apply one candidate to a proposed specification revision, retaining alternatives and rationale. Seventh, reassess affected boundaries without silently dropping requirements. Eighth, continue, ask for missing information, or return a scoped complete, partial, or blocked record. The exact selection and comparison policy is the central open design question.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "a587aba5-97f0-4a0d-9357-37a78a8a36ff",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. Actions and Applicability",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "ffca7511-0a95-4d99-b816-978c777566f3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The table below is an initial action vocabulary, not yet a complete decision table. Each action needs its own preconditions, output schema, failure states, and rule identity. Configuration and dependency references can be described without supplying live credentials or activating a resource.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
