    {
      "id": "39d10630-b844-47ee-82ff-7e6ccca4edfd",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "6. Comparing Alternatives without Hiding Assumptions",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "bd578e66-3380-4811-8090-3b77fdacf132",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mandatory-constraint checks precede preference ranking. A candidate that is unknown is not equivalent to one known to satisfy its conditions. Possible comparison dimensions include requirement relevance, unresolved assumptions, the scope of affected changes, applicable reusable evidence, expected specification progress, and estimated cost. Estimates must be distinguished from observations. We have not selected weights, a reward function, or a solver; an interpretable deterministic ordering is one candidate policy to formalize and challenge. Any policy must specify tie-breaking and retain rejected alternatives in the decision trace.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "07542270-a8e6-4f94-9e26-42af1a6d3d7b",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "7. Symbolic Example: An Incomplete Summarizer Specification",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "77c9c74d-b040-48af-bed7-d1efc000e509",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Suppose the intent requires a source-grounded three-point summary, and the available descriptions include Chat Input, Agent, and Chat Output. Their declared interfaces support the Message-valued connections, but that alone does not close the grounding requirement. The policy should distinguish a missing configuration binding from a missing capability or an unsupported behavioral claim. A symbolic revision may specify instructions and a model reference while carrying grounding and failure-handling obligations forward. No model needs to run to write that revision.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "a7dec559-008f-4fee-93a2-630510838e99",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "8. Stopping and Revision Obligations",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "1596a2e0-2343-4d56-b889-33da1a733128",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each attempted action consumes a positive unit of a finite design budget, and its suboperations must terminate or be interruptible. A complete claim is requirement-scoped and needs every mandatory judgment to meet its stated evidence criterion; untested behavior must not become met merely because its test plan is written. Budget exhaustion returns a partial specification with remaining gaps. Rejection concerns the current candidate; it does not establish that the intent is impossible. Revising scope or removing a requirement requires an explicit decision.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "9d35cb3c-86cd-45e9-8134-771300d3e884",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "9. Editorial Visualization Placeholders",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "42e8def0-2866-4803-855b-3c0f66dd9685",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "[FIGURE PLACEHOLDER P2-F1 — Boundary record: requirement above, available descriptions below, and the precise unsupported relation between them.] [FIGURE PLACEHOLDER P2-F2 — Decision flow: locate boundary → propose actions → judge conditions → compare → revise specification → reassess, with clarification and stopping branches.] [FIGURE PLACEHOLDER P2-F3 — Layered view showing downward decomposition, horizontal composition, and upward packaging as distinct alternatives.] [TABLE PLACEHOLDER P2-T1 — Complete action preconditions and output records, to be developed from the initial vocabulary below.] These are text annotations for future editorial production, not executable figures.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "29631caf-32f6-4d4d-8afd-f49c4eedfad7",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "10. Open Questions for the Next Draft",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "b3c26837-206f-41d0-adec-a08a2170e99a",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "What is the minimal boundary record? Which action families are applicable to each gap type? What must a candidate supply to support an expected progress claim? How do we rank unknowns without rewarding speculation? How should rules detect revisiting equivalent states? Which layer-specific requirements must remain fixed when a higher-layer record is rewritten? These questions must be answered before calling the policy a complete algorithm.",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "2819284d-7716-4fc4-a0a3-70e44affabb4",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left",
        "level": 2,
        "isToggleable": false
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "11. Review and Evidence Status",
          "styles": {}
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    {
      "id": "81b54904-9de7-415f-b3a8-7fa99010bc3f",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {
        "backgroundColor": "default",
        "textColor": "default",
        "textAlignment": "left"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
